# S3

Object storage over the S3 protocol — Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2, MinIO and the rest — signed with
AWS Signature Version 4 and sent with `httpclient.lg`, all in LOGOS.

A store is named by an `S3Store`: the endpoint (`https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com` for
R2), the region (`auto` for R2), the bucket and the key pair. Objects are addressed path-style,
`<endpoint>/<bucket>/<key>`. A request is signed over the host, the date and the payload hash;
uploads are sent with `UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD`, which every S3 service accepts over TLS, so a large body is
never hashed by the LOGOS SHA-256 only to tell the server what it already checks itself.

The signing steps are separate functions, each pinned to the worked example in Amazon's Signature
Version 4 documentation, so a mistake shows up as a wrong canonical request, a wrong string to sign
or a wrong signature — never as an opaque `403`.

## Note
Where objects live and the key pair that may reach them.

## A S3Store has:
    an endpoint, which is Text.
    a region, which is Text.
    a bucket, which is Text.
    an accessKey, which is Text.
    a secretKey, which is Text.

## Note
Lower-case hex of the SHA-256 of `t`'s UTF-8 bytes.

## To s3HashHex (t: Text) -> Text:
    Return bytesToHex(sha256(text_bytes(t))).

## Note
Each segment of an object key percent-encoded as RFC 3986 asks, the `/` between them kept.

## To s3EncodePath (path: Text) -> Text:
    Let parts be split(path, "/").
    Let mutable out be "".
    Let mutable first be true.
    Repeat for p in parts:
        If first:
            Set out to urlEncode(p).
            Set first to false.
        Otherwise:
            Set out to out + "/" + urlEncode(p).
    Return out.

## Note
`names` in byte order, by insertion — the order every canonical list of headers is written in.

## To s3SortTexts (names: Seq of Text) -> Seq of Text:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for name in names:
        Let mutable placed be a new Seq of Text.
        Let mutable inserted be false.
        Repeat for have in out:
            If inserted is equal to false and bytesCompare(text_bytes(name), text_bytes(have)) is less than 0:
                Push name to placed.
                Set inserted to true.
            Push have to placed.
        If inserted is equal to false:
            Push name to placed.
        Set out to placed.
    Return out.

## Note
The canonical request of Signature Version 4: method, path, query, headers, signed names, payload.

`headers` are the headers to sign, names in any case; they are lower-cased, their values trimmed,
and written in byte order of their names. `query` is the canonical query string already (empty for
a plain object request).

## To s3CanonicalRequest (method: Text) and (path: Text) and (query: Text) and (headers: Map of Text to Text) and (payloadHash: Text) -> Text:
    Let mutable lowered be a new Map of Text to Text.
    Let mutable names be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for (k, v) in headers:
        Let low be lower(k).
        Set item low of lowered to trim(v).
        Push low to names.
    Let ordered be s3SortTexts(names).
    Let mutable canonical be "".
    Let mutable signed be "".
    Repeat for name in ordered:
        Set canonical to canonical + name + ":" + (item name of lowered) + "\n".
        If signed is equal to "":
            Set signed to name.
        Otherwise:
            Set signed to signed + ";" + name.
    Return method + "\n" + s3EncodePath(path) + "\n" + query + "\n" + canonical + "\n" + signed + "\n" + payloadHash.

## Note
The signed header names of a header map, lower-cased, in byte order, joined with `;`.

## To s3SignedHeaders (headers: Map of Text to Text) -> Text:
    Let mutable names be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for (k, v) in headers:
        Push lower(k) to names.
    Let mutable signed be "".
    Repeat for name in s3SortTexts(names):
        If signed is equal to "":
            Set signed to name.
        Otherwise:
            Set signed to signed + ";" + name.
    Return signed.

## Note
The string to sign: the algorithm, the request time, the credential scope, the canonical request's hash.

## To s3StringToSign (amzDate: Text) and (region: Text) and (canonicalRequest: Text) -> Text:
    Let day be textUpTo(amzDate, 8).
    Return "AWS4-HMAC-SHA256\n" + amzDate + "\n" + day + "/" + region + "/s3/aws4_request\n" + s3HashHex(canonicalRequest).

## Note
The signing key: the secret, chained through HMAC-SHA-256 with the day, the region, `s3`, `aws4_request`.

## To s3SigningKey (secretKey: Text) and (day: Text) and (region: Text) -> Seq of Int:
    Let kDate be hmacSha256(text_bytes("AWS4" + secretKey), text_bytes(day)).
    Let kRegion be hmacSha256(kDate, text_bytes(region)).
    Let kService be hmacSha256(kRegion, text_bytes("s3")).
    Return hmacSha256(kService, text_bytes("aws4_request")).

## Note
The `Authorization` header for one request, from its canonical parts and the store's keys.

## To s3Authorization (store: S3Store) and (method: Text) and (path: Text) and (query: Text) and (headers: Map of Text to Text) and (payloadHash: Text) and (amzDate: Text) -> Text:
    Let canonical be s3CanonicalRequest(method, path, query, headers, payloadHash).
    Let toSign be s3StringToSign(amzDate, store's region, canonical).
    Let day be textUpTo(amzDate, 8).
    Let key be s3SigningKey(store's secretKey, day, store's region).
    Let signature be bytesToHex(hmacSha256(key, text_bytes(toSign))).
    Return "AWS4-HMAC-SHA256 Credential=" + store's accessKey + "/" + day + "/" + store's region + "/s3/aws4_request, SignedHeaders=" + s3SignedHeaders(headers) + ", Signature=" + signature.

## Note
`n` as at least `width` decimal digits, zero-padded on the left.

## To s3Pad (n: Int) and (width: Int) -> Text:
    Let mutable t be "{n}".
    While length of text_bytes(t) is less than width:
        Set t to "0" + t.
    Return t.

## Note
A Unix time in milliseconds as the `YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ` stamp Signature Version 4 dates a request with.

The civil date comes from the count of days since 1970 by Hinnant's days-to-civil algorithm, exact
for every day after the epoch.

## To s3AmzDate (millis: Int) -> Text:
    Let secs be millis // 1000.
    Let days be secs // 86400.
    Let rem be secs - days * 86400.
    Let hh be rem // 3600.
    Let mm be (rem - hh * 3600) // 60.
    Let ss be rem - hh * 3600 - mm * 60.
    Let z be days + 719468.
    Let era be z // 146097.
    Let doe be z - era * 146097.
    Let yoe be (doe - doe // 1460 + doe // 36524 - doe // 146096) // 365.
    Let mutable y be yoe + era * 400.
    Let doy be doe - (365 * yoe + yoe // 4 - yoe // 100).
    Let mp be (5 * doy + 2) // 153.
    Let d be doy - (153 * mp + 2) // 5 + 1.
    Let mutable m be mp + 3.
    If mp is at least 10:
        Set m to mp - 9.
    If m is at most 2:
        Set y to y + 1.
    Return s3Pad(y, 4) + s3Pad(m, 2) + s3Pad(d, 2) + "T" + s3Pad(hh, 2) + s3Pad(mm, 2) + s3Pad(ss, 2) + "Z".

## Note
The host of an endpoint URL: what lies between `://` and the next `/`.

## To s3Host (endpoint: Text) -> Text:
    Return cutBefore(cutAfter(endpoint, "://"), "/").

## Note
One signed request for the object `key`, sent now, under `timeoutMs`.

## To s3Request (store: S3Store) and (method: Text) and (key: Text) and (body: Text) and (contentType: Text) and (timeoutMs: Int) -> Result of HttpReply and Text:
    Let amzDate be s3AmzDate(now()).
    Let path be "/" + store's bucket + "/" + key.
    Let payloadHash be "UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD".
    Let mutable signedHs be a new Map of Text to Text.
    Set item "host" of signedHs to s3Host(store's endpoint).
    Set item "x-amz-content-sha256" of signedHs to payloadHash.
    Set item "x-amz-date" of signedHs to amzDate.
    Let auth be s3Authorization(store, method, path, "", signedHs, payloadHash, amzDate).
    Let mutable hs be a new Map of Text to Text.
    Set item "x-amz-content-sha256" of hs to payloadHash.
    Set item "x-amz-date" of hs to amzDate.
    Set item "Authorization" of hs to auth.
    If contentType is not "":
        Set item "Content-Type" of hs to contentType.
    Return httpRequest(method, store's endpoint + s3EncodePath(path), hs, body, timeoutMs).

## Note
Stores `body` as the object `key`. `Ok` carries the store's reply; a status other than 200 is the store refusing.

## To s3Put (store: S3Store) and (key: Text) and (body: Text) and (contentType: Text) and (timeoutMs: Int) -> Result of HttpReply and Text:
    Return s3Request(store, "PUT", key, body, contentType, timeoutMs).

## Note
Reads the object `key`. A missing object is an `Ok` whose status is 404.

## To s3Get (store: S3Store) and (key: Text) and (timeoutMs: Int) -> Result of HttpReply and Text:
    Return s3Request(store, "GET", key, "", "", timeoutMs).
